-- Platformer — gravity, a jump, and standing on things.
--
-- What this exercises that the ball games do not:
--   * acceleration. `speed` is constant, but a level rule that nudges
--     `ydirection` every tick *is* gravity, and `min` caps the terminal fall.
--   * `on press` as a discrete action rather than a held direction
--   * an object resting on another rather than bouncing off it
--   * a collectible that disappears and scores

start play

scene play

create object hero (width 1 cell, height 2 cells, speed 55vw, sprite hero.svg)
create object ledge (width 25vw, height 1 cell, sprite ledge.svg)
create object coin (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, sprite coin.svg)

create hero player in play (x 15vw, y 2)

-- A full-width floor would be a wall of hardware sprites (20 of a Game Boy's 40
-- for the floor alone), and the compiler says so. Static scenery really wants a
-- background layer, which the language does not have yet — so for now the floor
-- is a ledge you can fall off, and `screenbottom` catches you.
create ledge floor in play (x 10vw, y screenheight - 1, width 55vw)
create ledge shelf1 in play (x 20vw, y screenheight - 5)
create ledge shelf2 in play (x 60vw, y screenheight - 9)

create coin coin1 in play (x 28vw, y screenheight - 7)
create coin coin2 in play (x 68vw, y screenheight - 11)

create number score in play (value 0, x 1, y 1)

-- Gravity: a constant downward nudge, clamped so the fall has a terminal speed.
-- Without the clamp a long drop tunnels through the floor in one tick, which the
-- compiler would tell you about.
when always in play then player.ydirection as min(player.ydirection + 0.04, 0.9)

control player left (xdirection -1) on hold
control player right (xdirection 1) on hold

-- The jump is an impulse, not a held direction: `on press` fires once per press
-- however long the button is held down.
control player a (ydirection -0.85) on press

when player hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0
when player touches screenbottom then ydirection as 0

-- Landing. This is `touches`, not `hits`, and the difference is the whole reason
-- `touches` exists: `hits` fires once, on contact, so gravity would go on adding
-- to `ydirection` every tick the hero stood still. The separation would keep it
-- looking correct while the state ran away underneath — and the first jump after
-- a long stand would be fought by a full tick of accumulated fall.
when player touches ledge then ydirection as 0

when player hits coin then (coin.visible, score.value) as (0, score.value + 1)
